Chow-style pooled interaction (1991-2015, T=82, levels): prim interaction -21.22 (p=0.0002), sec interaction +10.57 (p=0.331). Robustness only — it inherits the level trend problem.
**The negative level coefficients are the timing artifact, not the finding.** SSA savings share peaked at **6.10% in 1997** and fell to 3.15% by 2015 (-2.94 pp) while enrollment accelerated after the peak: primary **+0.25 -> +1.33 pp/yr**, secondary **+0.41 -> +1.25 pp/yr**. LMC peaked 3.87% in 1999; post-peak primary +0.60 vs +0.48, secondary +1.32 vs +0.79 pp/yr. Enrollment grew faster when the education share was falling in both groups.

## 5. Required denominator check (share vs base)
| Quantity | Group | RAW level | Linear-detrended | First difference |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| corr(log GNIpc, log **share**) | LMC | -0.417 (T=41) | -0.551 | +0.197 (T=40) |
| corr(log GNIpc, log **share**) | SSF | -0.970 (T=5) | -0.145 | -0.489 (T=4) |
| corr(log GNIpc, log **per-capita flow = share x GNIpc**) | LMC | +0.958 (T=41) | +0.622 | +0.506 (T=40) |
Because the exposure is a ratio **to GNI**, the outcome sits in its denominator. Removing that shared base flips the LMC association from -0.42 (levels) / +0.20 (differenced) to +0.51/+0.62. Detrending the share alone does not fix the problem; reformulating as an absolute per-capita flow does. Differencing reduces but does not eliminate the link, so the share-based numbers are not interpretable as effects.

## 6. SSA income leg — descriptive only, deliberately no p-values
